    "response": "The `can_omit_invisible_parens` function determines whether optional parentheses around a given expression can be safely omitted without affecting the readability or functionality of the code. It takes two parameters: `rhs`, an instance of `RHSResult` representing the right-hand side of an expression, and `line_length`, an integer specifying the maximum allowed line length. The function returns a boolean value indicating whether the parentheses can be omitted.\n\nThe function first checks for standalone comments that are not enclosed in parentheses, in which case it returns `False`. It then examines the bracket structure of the expression, considering the priority and count of delimiters. If there are multiple delimiters of the same high priority, it returns `False`. For a single delimiter, it checks if the expression starts or ends with a bracket and uses helper functions `_can_omit_opening_paren` and `_can_omit_closing_paren` to determine if the parentheses can be omitted based on the line length and the nature of the brackets. If the expression is a single method call, it returns `True`. The function handles edge cases such as empty brackets and multiline strings, and it asserts that the line has at least two leaves to avoid errors. There are no side effects; the function only queries the input objects and returns a boolean value.",
